# paint/scene.planes — the vocabulary a child is given in week 1 (checkpoint
# v26.0 §383-407, checkpoint v27.0 §413-416). This is a TEACHING vocabulary:
# one wrapper per idea and nothing else, the same discipline paint/draw.planes
# follows over the protocol itself. Every drawing command below goes through a
# draw.planes helper — this file defines no protocol surface of its own.
#
# v27.0's RENDER CEILING (§413): the child's one-line call stays exactly as
# simple as before — `sun of 240, 70` is still two numbers — but the helper
# behind it now spends the FULL draw.planes palette: gradients, shadows,
# blur, curved paths. The richness lives in here, never in what she types.
#
# THE ONE BOUNDARY (§414, checked by js/test/scene_rich.test.mjs's exactness
# control test): nothing below calls `sine` or `root`, or anything that
# reaches them. A hexagonal ring of six petals needs the sine and cosine of
# 60-degree steps, and those are baked in as DECIMAL LITERALS (`6.928` for
# 8 * sin(60°)) rather than computed by the builtin — a literal is an exact
# rational by construction (grammar/vocabulary.json's NUMBER token), where
# calling `sine` would flip this program's effect surface from exact to
# approximate and break week 5's honest-garden lesson. Six fixed angles
# needed six fixed numbers, not a trig call.
#
# THE SUBJECT-ATTRIBUTION RULE (§415) needs no code here at all. Every helper
# below draws only by calling further scene.planes/draw.planes functions —
# never anything the entry program calls on its own — so interp.py/interp.mjs's
# own `trace_line` (every `show` inside a `use`d module reports the innermost
# call site actually WRITTEN in the entry file) already collapses a rich
# helper's many marks onto the ONE line the child wrote, and tutor.html's
# existing call-name heading reads that line's name straight off it. A second,
# hand-maintained subject map (garden.html's `subjectOf`) is not needed here
# because scene.planes is always `use`d, never the entry file itself.
#
# Its OKLCH numbers (`fill`/`stroke`/`background`/`gradient`'s l, c, h, a) are
# the only colour literals a child's own program ever has to look at; her
# program only ever writes phrases like "wet grass" and coordinates like
# `120, 90`. The sky/ground phrase lists below are the same list
# js/scene_vocab.mjs exports for the read-only key (checkpoint v27.0 §421) —
# js/test/scene_rich.test.mjs asserts the two agree, phrase for phrase.

use draw

# `horizon` is a zero-argument FUNCTION, not a top-level `let` — a `let` at a
# used module's top level is hoisted as a declaration but its right-hand side
# never runs (interp.py's `hoist` only registers function definitions; a
# module's own top-level statements execute only when IT is the entry file,
# and scene.planes is always `use`d, never the entry). A function is hoisted
# and callable regardless, so the y-coordinate where the sky ends and the
# ground begins — shared by `ground`'s own band and by every flower's stem —
# is written once here rather than twice.
to horizon:
  give 264

# The protocol has no verb to turn a shadow or a blur back off (paint/
# garden.planes's own §"shadow lifecycle" names the same gap) — a shadow or
# blur set for one mark keeps applying to every mark drawn after it until
# something changes it again. These two private resets exist so a helper that
# wants a glow or a lift on ONE mark can hand the state back afterward,
# instead of leaking a blurred, shadowed sun onto the ground drawn beneath it.
to no-shadow:
  shadow of 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0

to no-blur:
  blur of 0

to start:
  show "draw protocol 3"
  clear
  # No `stroke of` call below ever sets one back to opaque black, so every
  # filled shape in this file — sun, moon, flower petals, a bee's body — would
  # otherwise carry the DEFAULT stroke (draw.planes's own table: black, width
  # 1) as an uninvited outline. Set once, globally, for the whole stream.
  stroke of 0, 0, 0, 0

to sky of feeling:
  if feeling == "early morning":
    gradient of "linear", 0, 0, 0, 0, horizon, 0.80, 0.07, 235, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.82, 0.05, 40, 1
  else:
    if feeling == "middle of the afternoon":
      gradient of "linear", 0, 0, 0, 0, horizon, 0.55, 0.18, 235, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.75, 0.12, 230, 1
    else:
      if feeling == "just before dark":
        gradient of "linear", 0, 0, 0, 0, horizon, 0.25, 0.13, 285, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.45, 0.14, 300, 1
      else:
        if feeling == "the middle of the night":
          gradient of "linear", 0, 0, 0, 0, horizon, 0.08, 0.03, 265, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.15, 0.05, 260, 1
        else:
          fail "\"" + feeling + "\" isn't a sky this program knows — try one of: early morning, middle of the afternoon, just before dark, the middle of the night" as unknown-sky-feeling
  rect of 0, 0, 480, horizon, 0

to ground of feeling:
  if feeling == "wet grass":
    gradient of "linear", 0, 0, horizon, 0, 360, 0.58, 0.12, 145, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.40, 0.13, 145, 1
  else:
    if feeling == "dry grass":
      gradient of "linear", 0, 0, horizon, 0, 360, 0.75, 0.08, 95, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.62, 0.09, 95, 1
    else:
      if feeling == "dirt":
        gradient of "linear", 0, 0, horizon, 0, 360, 0.55, 0.09, 55, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.40, 0.08, 55, 1
      else:
        fail "\"" + feeling + "\" isn't a ground this program knows — try one of: wet grass, dry grass, dirt" as unknown-ground-feeling
  rect of 0, horizon, 480, (360 - horizon), 0

# A raw-number sky, for a child's OWN `to <her words>:` sky (checkpoint
# v27.0 §420/§424, week 4): she names the function, and tutor.html's colour
# picker writes only the three numbers into a `custom-sky of l, c, h` line
# inside it — the authorship boundary the checkpoint locks. Unlike `sky`,
# this takes no phrase and refuses nothing: any OKLCH triple is a colour.
to custom-sky of l, c, h:
  fill of l, c, h, 1
  rect of 0, 0, 480, horizon, 0

to sun of x, y:
  fill of 0.97, 0.14, 95, 0.55
  blur of 14
  circle of x, y, 46
  no-blur
  shadow of 0, 3, 6, 0.70, 0.14, 70
  gradient of "radial", 0, x, y, 27, 0, 0.99, 0.05, 95, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.86, 0.17, 68, 1
  circle of x, y, 27
  no-shadow

to moon of x, y:
  fill of 0.90, 0.03, 250, 0.5
  blur of 12
  circle of x, y, 36
  no-blur
  shadow of 0, 2, 5, 0.30, 0.04, 260
  gradient of "radial", 0, x, y, 20, 0, 0.97, 0.01, 250, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.80, 0.03, 245, 1
  circle of x, y, 20
  no-shadow
  fill of 0.72, 0.02, 245, 0.7
  circle of (x + 6), (y - 4), 3.4
  circle of (x - 5), (y + 5), 2.6

to star of x, y:
  fill of 0.85, 0.18, 95, 0.5
  blur of 3
  circle of x, y, 5
  no-blur
  fill of 0.99, 0.05, 95, 1
  circle of x, y, 2

# One petal, at its own centre, rotated to point outward. A private helper
# so `flower` states six short calls rather than repeating an eighteen-
# argument `gradient of` six times over.
to petal of cx, cy, turn:
  gradient of "radial", 0, cx, cy, 7, 0, 0.92, 0.16, 24, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.72, 0.19, 20, 1
  ellipse of cx, cy, 7, 4.2, turn

to flower of x, tall:
  let stem-top = (horizon - tall)
  let stem-mid = ((horizon + stem-top) / 2)
  fill of 0.42, 0.12, 145, 1
  shape
  vertex of (x - 1.6), horizon
  curve of (x - 3.4), stem-mid, (x - 1.2), (stem-top + 6), x, stem-top
  curve of (x + 1.2), (stem-top + 6), (x + 3.4), stem-mid, (x + 1.6), horizon
  close
  end
  fill of 0.48, 0.14, 140, 1
  shape
  vertex of x, stem-mid
  curve of (x + 10), (stem-mid - 6), (x + 13), (stem-mid + 2), (x + 6), (stem-mid + 7)
  curve of (x + 3), (stem-mid + 5), (x + 1), (stem-mid + 3), x, stem-mid
  close
  end
  shadow of 0, 2, 4, 0.32, 0.10, 25
  # Six petals in a ring, radius 8, at 60-degree steps — the ring's own
  # (dx, dy) offsets baked in as literals (see the file header's note on why).
  petal of (x + 8), stem-top, 0
  petal of (x + 4), (stem-top - 6.928), 60
  petal of (x - 4), (stem-top - 6.928), 120
  petal of (x - 8), stem-top, 180
  petal of (x - 4), (stem-top + 6.928), 240
  petal of (x + 4), (stem-top + 6.928), 300
  no-shadow
  gradient of "radial", 0, x, stem-top, 5, 0, 1, 0.02, 95, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.82, 0.16, 70, 1
  circle of x, stem-top, 5

to bee of x, y:
  shadow of 0, 1, 2, 0.30, 0.08, 70
  gradient of "radial", 0, x, y, 6, 0, 0.85, 0.16, 90, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.68, 0.17, 80, 1
  ellipse of x, y, 6, 4.4, 0
  no-shadow
  fill of 0.25, 0.05, 60, 1
  rect of (x - 4), (y - 4.6), 2.2, 9.2, 0
  rect of (x + 1.8), (y - 4.6), 2.2, 9.2, 0
  fill of 1, 0.02, 220, 0.5
  ellipse of (x - 3), (y - 4), 4.2, 3, -18
  ellipse of (x + 3), (y - 4), 4.2, 3, 18

to firefly of x, y:
  fill of 0.90, 0.16, 100, 0.45
  blur of 6
  circle of x, y, 7
  no-blur
  gradient of "radial", 0, x, y, 2.6, 0, 1, 0.06, 100, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0.85, 0.20, 95, 1
  circle of x, y, 2.6

# Paired helpers (checkpoint v27.0 §416): symmetry is the helper's doing, so
# a later week can change the shared height or move just one flower — the
# child still writes one line. Offsets match mockup v7's own placement.
to two-flowers of x, tall:
  flower of (x - 26), tall
  flower of (x + 26), tall

to two-bees of x, y:
  bee of (x - 22), (y - 6)
  bee of (x + 22), (y + 8)

to two-fireflies of x, y:
  firefly of (x - 22), (y - 6)
  firefly of (x + 22), (y + 8)
